-3/2 and 5. Their sum is 7/2. Equivalently, the expanded quadratic is 4x^2-14x-30, whose root sum is 14/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_150"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_10A_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_150","cnm_042","cnm_039","cnm_010"],"id":"solution:factor-and-find-roots:f3ab7fb23c19","label":"Factor the repeated expression","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-find-roots":["cnm_151","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_151","cnm_224","cnm_150","cnm_042","cnm_039","cnm_010"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:7767d43cedf134eacc7f780f"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: Three lines","reason":"The equality becomes 2xy(x-y)=0, equivalent to x=0, y=0, or x=y. These are three distinct lines.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_150"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_10B_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_150"],"id":"factor-coordinate-locus","label":"Factor the coordinate condition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-coordinate-locus":["math:defined-binary-operation-evaluation","cnm_036","cnm_151","cnm_h171"]},"nodeIds":["math:defined-binary-operation-evaluation","cnm_036","cnm_151","cnm_h171","cnm_082","cnm_150"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:7767d43cedf134eacc7f780f"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 16.5","reason":"The equation factors as (x-b)(2x-a-c)=0, so the root sum is (a+2b+c)/2. Distinct one-digit numbers satisfy a+b+c<=24 and b<=9, giving a sum at most 33/2. Taking b=9 and {a,c}={7,8} attains 16.5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_150"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_12B_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_150","cnm_042","cnm_096"],"id":"factor-and-bound","label":"Expose the roots and bound their sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-bound":["cnm_151","cnm_224","math:extremal-principle"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_151","cnm_224","math:extremal-principle","cnm_150","cnm_042","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:7767d43cedf134eacc7f780f"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 6","reason":"Since n^3-n=(n-1)n(n+1), it contains an even factor and a multiple of 3, so 6 always divides it. At n=2 the expression equals 6, ruling out any larger universal divisor.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_150"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1951_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_150","cnm_147","cnm_p156"],"id":"solution:consecutive-integer-factors:b19008412df8","label":"Factor into consecutive integers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"consecutive-integer-factors":["cnm_151","cnm_152","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_151","cnm_152","cnm_155","cnm_150","cnm_147","cnm_p156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:7767d43cedf134eacc7f780f"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 4","reason":"Subtracting three times the first polynomial from the second gives -14(x^2-2x+5). Thus the monic quadratic p is x^2-2x+5; it divides both given polynomials, and p(1) = 4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_150"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1986_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_150","cnm_029"],"id":"eliminate-quartic-terms","label":"Eliminate the fourth-degree terms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"eliminate-quartic-terms":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_036","cnm_145","cnm_146"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_036","cnm_145","cnm_146","cnm_032","cnm_150","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:7767d43cedf134eacc7f780f"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, (ax+by)², provided bx+ay ≠ 0","reason":"The numerator is (bx+ay)(a²x²+b²y²)+2abxy(bx+ay) = (bx+ay)(ax+by)². Dividing by bx+ay gives (ax+by)² on the original domain bx+ay ≠ 0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_150"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1988_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_157","cnm_148","cnm_150"],"id":"common-part-factorization","label":"Collect the shared terms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-part-factorization":["cnm_158","cnm_151","cnm_153"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_158","cnm_151","cnm_153","cnm_156","cnm_157","cnm_148","cnm_150"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:7767d43cedf134eacc7f780f"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (10)","reason":"The equations become (a+1)(b+1)=525, (b+1)(c+1)=147, and (c+1)(d+1)=105. Their positive integer possibilities are (a,b,c,d)=(24,20,6,14) and (74,6,20,4). Only the first has product 40320=8!, giving a-d=10.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_150"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2001_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_150","cnm_040"],"id":"shift-and-divisors","label":"Add one and use common divisors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shift-and-divisors":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_151","cnm_p158","cnm_p153","math:factorial-notation"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_151","cnm_p158","cnm_p153","math:factorial-notation","cnm_150","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:7767d43cedf134eacc7f780f"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 1","reason":"Positivity and finite sums imply 0<r_j<1. Each ratio satisfies r_j²-r_j+a=0. Since the series are different, r_1 and r_2 are its two distinct roots, whose sum is 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_150"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_147","cnm_150","cnm_040"],"id":"solution:subtract-and-factor:7f03d9653894","label":"Eliminate the common first term","methodDirectNodeIds":{"subtract-and-factor":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_152","cnm_151"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_152","cnm_151","cnm_h207","cnm_147","cnm_150","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:7767d43cedf134eacc7f780f"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (11)","reason":"Let n=AC=AB and m=AD. Then (n-m)(n+m)=57. The positive factor pairs (1,57) and (3,19) give n=29 and n=11 respectively. The latter has AD=8 and CD=3, so the minimum 11 is feasible.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_150"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1999_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_064","cnm_075","cnm_147","cnm_150"],"id":"difference-of-squares","label":"Use factor pairs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"difference-of-squares":["cnm_174","cnm_152","math:integer-diophantine-equations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_152","math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_055","cnm_064","cnm_075","cnm_147","cnm_150"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"alignmentIds":["alignment:7767d43cedf134eacc7f780f"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 802","reason":"Factor f(n) = (n^2 + 20n + 20)(n^2 - 20n + 20). The first factor exceeds 1 for every positive integer n, so a positive prime value requires n^2 - 20n + 20 = 1. This gives (n - 10)^2 = 81, hence n = 1 or 19. The corresponding values are the primes 41 and 761, whose sum is 802.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_150"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNode